## 4. The extra-payment strategy (this changes the whole decision)

SoFi confirmed — and its published policy states — there are **no prepayment penalties**, no application fees, and no origination fees. Extra payments go straight at the balance. This has a big strategic consequence:

**The required payment is a floor, not a ceiling. So the real question is: what is the lowest payment you can *always* make, even in a bad month?**

Because all five rates are close (5.00%–6.01%), the plan mostly determines your *obligation*, not your destiny. Two illustrations from the simulation:

If you take the **20-year plan ($280.78 required)** but actually pay $738.96 every month, you pay it off in 5.2 years with $6,462 interest — only ~$1,279 more than the actual 5-year plan, while keeping the right to drop to $280.78 any month things get tight. That flexibility costs roughly $21/month of extra interest.

If you take the **10-year plan ($425.11 required)** and pay $738.96, you finish in about 5.1 years with ~$5,752 interest — only ~$570 more than the 5-year plan, with a much safer floor.

The decision rule that falls out of the math: **choose the plan with the lowest rate whose *required* payment you can guarantee in your worst realistic month, then throw every spare dollar at it.** Since lower rate always wins at any given actual payment, never pick a longer term than your safety floor requires. For most budgets, the 7-year (5.21%) or 10-year (5.51%) plan is the sweet spot for a drilling Guard member with variable income: a survivable floor, a near-best rate.

Also stack the discounts: SoFi's **0.25% autopay** rate reduction (and a further 0.125% with SoFi Plus direct deposit) applies to refinance loans — ask whether your quoted rates already include it. On $39,155, 0.25% is roughly $95/year at the start.

