## 6. National Guard + student: verified policies and better options

You asked me to verify the policies and research whether there is a better option for someone who is in the National Guard and a student at the same time. Here is what checks out, what doesn't apply, and what to do about it.

**SoFi policy verification — confirmed.** SoFi charges no application fee, no origination fee, and no prepayment penalty on student loan refinancing; fixed-rate options are real and the rate never changes; the minimum refinance amount is $5,000; loans are serviced by MOHELA; a 0.25% autopay discount is available. One honest limitation: a SoFi loan is still a private loan, so it will never have income-driven repayment or federal forgiveness — but your Sallie Mae loans are private too, so **you are not giving up any federal benefits by doing this refinance.** That is the trap that catches most people, and it does not apply to you.

**SCRA 6% interest cap — read this before you sign.** Under the Servicemembers Civil Relief Act, debt you incurred *before* entering military service is capped at 6% interest during periods of qualifying active duty — and this explicitly covers private student loans and National Guard members on Title 10 orders or qualifying Title 32 orders over 30 days. Two implications for you. First, if you took the Sallie Mae loans before you joined the Guard and you get activated, you could demand those 9.7–11.25% rates drop to 6% for the duration of orders (send the lender written notice plus a copy of orders, up to 180 days after service ends; excess interest must be refunded). Second — and this is the key nuance — **refinancing creates a new loan originated during your service, which is generally not eligible for the SCRA cap.** In your case this barely matters, because your new fixed rates (5.00–6.01%) are already at or below the 6% cap, permanently, without needing orders. But if you were choosing the 20-year 6.01% plan *and* expected long activations, know that you're trading away a conditional 6% cap for a permanent 6.01% rate. If you are activated *right now* on qualifying orders, consider invoking SCRA on the Sallie Mae loans first and refinancing after you demobilize.

**Army National Guard Student Loan Repayment Program (SLRP) — the catch that matters most.** The Guard's SLRP can repay up to $50,000 (paid annually, up to ~$8,333/year per current terms) for soldiers who contract into a critical-skill vacancy for a 6- or 8-year term, with payments sent directly to the lender. **But SLRP covers qualifying *federal* loans — private Sallie Mae or SoFi loans are generally not eligible.** So SLRP unfortunately does not rescue this particular debt. If you ever re-enlist or extend, talk to your unit's education/incentives office *before* signing, because SLRP eligibility is locked in at contract time — but do not delay this refinance waiting on it.

**Oklahoma-specific benefits — use these for the "student" half.** Since you're in Oklahoma: the **Oklahoma National Guard Educational Assistance Program (SB 1418, effective 2022)** waives resident tuition and mandatory academic fees at Oklahoma public colleges and universities for active Guard members, up to 18 credit hours per semester, requiring a 2.0 GPA and satisfactory Guard participation. Stack that with **Federal Tuition Assistance** and the **Montgomery GI Bill – Selected Reserve (Chapter 1606)** monthly stipend. Translation: while you remain in the Guard, you should not need to borrow another dollar for tuition in Oklahoma — the biggest "better option" available to you isn't a lower rate, it's making sure this $38,599 is the *last* student debt you ever take on.

**Is there a better lender than SoFi?** SoFi's terms (no fees, no prepayment penalty, fixed 5.00–6.01% for your profile) are competitive. Before signing, it costs nothing but soft credit pulls to rate-check two or three others in the same week: **Navy Federal Credit Union** and **PenFed** (both serve Guard members; PenFed also has no fees), plus **Earnest** or **Laurel Road**, and **Credible/Bankrate** style marketplaces to see multiple quotes at once. If nobody beats 5.00–5.51% fixed for your term, take the SoFi offer with autopay enabled. Do not slow-roll this — every month of delay at 10.8% costs you about $347 minus what a refinanced month would cost, roughly **$165–$170 of pure waste per month**.

